    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "Given that $n$ is such that when divided by 6, a remainder of 1 is found.\nDetermine $(n+2010) \\div 6$ and find the remainder of $n+2010$ when it is divided by 6.\n\nTo solve this problem, we need to determine the remainder when \\( n+2010 \\) is divided by 6, given that \\( n \\) gives a remainder of 1 when divided by 6.\n\n1. **Understand the Remainder Condition:**\n   \\[\n   n \\equiv 1 \\pmod{6}\n   \\]\n   This means that \\( n \\) can be written as:\n   \\[\n   n = 6k + 1 \\quad \\text{for some integer } k.\n   \\]\n\n2. **Express \\( n + 2010 \\) in terms of \\( n \\):**\n   \\[\n   n + 2010 = (6k + 1) + 2010 = 6k + 2011\n   \\]\n\n3. **Simplify \\( 2011 \\) modulo 6:**\n   To find \\( 2011 \\) modulo 6, we first determine the remainder of 2010 modulo 6:\n   \\[\n   2010 \\div 6 = 335 \\quad \\text{remainder} \\quad 0\n   \\]\n   Thus,\n   \\[\n   2010 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{6}\n   \\]\n   Now, add the quotient from the division:\n   \\[\n   2011 \\equiv 2011 - 2010 \\pmod{6}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   2011 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{6}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Calculate \\( n + 2010 \\mod 6 \\):**\n   Given that \\( n \\equiv 1 \\pmod{6} \\),\n   \\[\n   n + 2010 \\equiv 1 + 1 \\pmod{6}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   n + 2010 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{6}\n   \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\nThe remainder when \\( n+2010 \\) is divided by 6 is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).",
